{"subjectId":"dsa-advanced","id":"dp","title":"Dynamic Programming","group":"","order":19,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Dynamic Programming solves problems by combining solutions to overlapping subproblems, using Memoization (Top-down) or Tabulation (Bottom-up).</p><div>\n<p>In simple words, it is an optimization over plan recursion. The idea is to reuse the solution of subproblem when there are overlapping subproblem.</p>\n<p>1. memoization (Top down)</p>\n<p>2. Tabulation (Bootom up)</p>\n<h4>Applications</h4>\n<p>1. Bellman Ford Algorithm</p>\n<p>2. Floyd Warshall Algorithm</p>\n<p>3. Diff utility (LCS : Longest Common Subsequence)</p>\n<p>4. Search cloud Words (Edit Distanct)</p>\n<p>5. Resource Allocation (0-1 Knapsack)</p>\n<h3>Memoization</h3>\n<p>Example : Fibonacci Numbers</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> n = 3<br><strong>Output:</strong> 2</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Implementation\n\ndef fib(n):\n  if n == 0 or n == 1:\n    return n\n\n  return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)\n\nfib(3)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>-&gt; A Simple Recursive solution with exponential time complexity</p>\n<p>-&gt; we are going to optimize it using memoization</p>\n<h4>Observations on the above Recursion Tree</h4>\n<p>-&gt; we solve some subproblems again : fib(3) : 2 Times; fib(2) : 3 times</p>\n<p>-&gt; Memoization Idea : Store solutions and before proceeding further, check if already completed</p>\n<p>NOTE : So that we have 2n-1 function calls now</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\nmemo = [None] * 100\n\ndef fib(n):\n  if memo[n] != None:\n    return memo[n]\n\n  if n == 0 or n == 1:\n    memo[n] = n\n  else:\n    memo[n] = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)\n\n  return memo[n]\n\nfib(3)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Tabulation : A Bottom-Up Way</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Implementation\n\ndef fib(n):\n  dp = [None] * (n+1)\n  dp[0] = 0\n  dp[1] = 1\n\n  for i in range(2, n+1):\n    dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2]\n\n  return dp[n]\n\nfib(3)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Longest Common Subsequence</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> s1 = \"ABCDGH\"; s2 = \"AEDFHR\"<br><strong>Output:</strong> 3<br><strong>Input:</strong> s1 = \"xyz\"; s2 = \"xyz\"<br><strong>Output:</strong> 3</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursion\n\ndef lcsR(s1, s2, n, m):\n  if m == 0 or n == 0:\n    return 0\n\n  if s1[n-1] == s2[m-1]:\n    return 1 + lcsR(s1, s2, n-1, m-1)\n  else:\n    return max(lcsR(s1, s2, n, m-1), lcsR(s1, s2, n-1, m))\n\ns1 = \"ABCDGH\"\ns2 = \"AEDFHR\"\nlcsR(s1, s2, len(s1), len(s2))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Memoization\n\nM = 1000\nN = 1000\n\nmemo = [[-1] * N for i in range(M)]\n\ndef lcsM(s1, s2, n, m):\n  if memo[n][m] != -1:\n    return memo[n][m]\n\n  if n == 0 or m == 0:\n    memo[n][m] = 0\n  else:\n    if s1[n-1] == s2[m-1]:\n      memo[n][m] = 1 + lcsM(s1, s2, n-1, m-1)\n    else:\n      memo[n][m] = max(lcsM(s1, s2, n, m-1), lcsM(s1, s2, n-1, m))\n\n  return memo[n][m]\n\ns1 = \"ABCDGH\"\ns2 = \"AEDFHR\"\nlcsM(s1, s2, len(s1), len(s2))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Tabulation\n\ndef lcsT(s1, s2):\n  m = len(s1)\n  n = len(s2)\n\n  dp = [[0 for j in range(n + 1)] for i in range(m + 1)]\n\n  for i in range(1, m + 1):\n    for j in range(1, n + 1):\n      if s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]:\n        dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1]\n      else:\n        dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n\n  return dp[m][n]\n\ns1 = \"ABCDGH\"\ns2 = \"AEDFHR\"\nlcsT(s1, s2)</code></pre></figure>\n<p> Variations of LCS</p>\n<p> 1. Diff Utility</p>\n<p> 2. minimum insertions and deletions to convert s1 into s2</p>\n<p> 3. shortest common super sequence</p>\n<p> 4. Longest Palindrome subsequence</p>\n<p> 5. Longest Repeating subsequence</p>\n<p> 6. Space Optimized DP solution of LCS</p>\n<p> 7. Printing LCS</p>\n<h3>Coin Change</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> coins = [1, 2, 3]<br><strong>Output:</strong> 4 ==&gt; [1+1+1+1, 2+2, 1+3, 2+1+1]</p>\n</div>\n<p> Idea for the Recursive solution</p>\n<p> coins = x0, x1, x2, ...... xn-1]</p>\n<p> sum = s</p>\n<p> we consider Two choices for every coin</p>\n<p> -&gt; Included</p>\n<p> -&gt; Not Included</p>\n<p> we return sum of the results of the Recursive calls</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursive\n\ndef countWays(coin, n, s):\n    if s == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    if s &lt; 0:\n        return 0\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    return countWays(coin, n, s - coin[n - 1]) + countWays(coin, n - 1, s)\n\n\ncoin = [1, 2, 3]\ns = 4\nn = len(coin)\nresult = countWays(coin, n, s)\nprint(result)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Tabulation\n\ndef countWaysDP(coin, s):\n    n = len(coin)\n\n    dp = [[0 for _ in range(s + 1)] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        dp[i][0] = 1\n\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        for j in range(1, s + 1):\n            dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j]\n\n            if j &gt;= coin[i - 1]:\n                dp[i][j] += dp[i][j - coin[i - 1]]\n\n    return dp[n][s]\n\ncoin = [1, 2, 3]\ns = 4\nresult = countWaysDP(coin, s)\nprint(result)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Edit Distance</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> s1 = \"CAT\"; s2 = \"CUT\"<br><strong>Output:</strong> 1</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursive\n\ndef ed(s1, s2, m, n):\n  if m == 0:\n    return n\n\n  if n == 0:\n    return m\n\n  if s1[m-1] == s2[m-1]:\n    return ed(s1, s2, m-1, n-1)\n  else:\n    return 1 + min(ed(s1, s2, m, n-1), ed(s1, s2, m-1, n), ed(s1, s2, m-1, n-1))\n\ns1 = \"CAT\"\ns2 = \"CUT\"\ned(s1, s2, len(s1), len(s2))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Tabulation\n\ndef edDP(s1, s2, m, n):\n  dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for i in range(m+1)]\n\n  for i in range(m+1):\n    dp[i][0] = i\n\n  for j in range(n+1):\n    dp[0][j] = j\n\n  for i in range(1, m+1):\n    for j in range(1, n+1):\n      if s1[i-1] == s2[j-1]:\n        dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]\n      else:\n        dp[i][j] = 1 + min(dp[i][j-1], dp[i-1][j], dp[i-1][j-1])\n\n  return dp[m][n]\n\ns1 = \"CAT\"\ns2 = \"CUT\"\nedDP(s1, s2, len(s1), len(s2))</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Longest Increasing Subsequence</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> arr = [3, 4, 2, 8, 10]<br><strong>Output:</strong> 4</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef LIS(arr):\n  n = len(arr)\n  lis = [1] * n\n\n  for i in range(1, n):\n    for j in range(0, i):\n      if arr[i] &gt; arr[j]:\n        lis[i] = max(lis[i], lis[j] + 1)\n\n  res = lis[0]\n\n  for i in range(n):\n    res = max(lis[i], res)\n\n  return res\n\narr = [3, 4, 2, 8, 10]\nLIS(arr)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Longest Increasing Subsequence in O(n logn)</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> arr = [8, 100, 150, 10, 12, 14, 110]<br><strong>Output:</strong> 5</p>\n</div>\n<p>tail[i] = stores the minimum possible tail value for LIS of length(i+1)</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef LISDP(arr):\n  n = len(arr)\n  tail = [arr[0]]\n\n  for i in range(1, n):\n    if arr[i] &gt; tail[-1]:\n      tail.append(arr[i])\n    else:\n      c = ceilIDK(tail, arr[i])\n      tail[c] = arr[i]\n\n  return len(tail)\n\ndef ceilIDK(tail, u):\n  l = 0\n  r = len(tail) - 1\n\n  while r &gt; l:\n    m = (l + (r-l)) // 2\n    if tail[m] &gt;= u:\n      r = m\n    else:\n      l = m + 1\n\n  return r\n\narr = [3, 4, 2, 8, 10]\nLISDP(arr)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Variations of LIS</h3>\n<p> 1. Minimum deletions to make an array sorted</p>\n<p> 2. Maximum sum increasing subsequence</p>\n<p> 3. Maximum length Bitonic subsequence</p>\n<p> 4. Building Bridges</p>\n<p> 5. The longest chain</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Minimum deletions to make an array sorted\n\ndef minDeletionsToSort(arr):\n    n = len(arr)\n    lis = [1] * n\n\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] &gt; arr[j] and lis[i] &lt; lis[j] + 1:\n                lis[i] = lis[j] + 1\n\n    return n - max(lis)\n\narr = [4, 3, 2, 6, 7, 8]\nprint(minDeletionsToSort(arr))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Maximum Sum Increasing Subsequence\n\ndef maxSumIncreasingSubsequence(arr):\n    n = len(arr)\n    msis = arr[:]\n\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] &gt; arr[j] and msis[i] &lt; msis[j] + arr[i]:\n                msis[i] = msis[j] + arr[i]\n\n    return max(msis)\n\n\narr = [1, 101, 2, 3, 100, 4, 5]\nprint(maxSumIncreasingSubsequence(arr))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Maximum Length Bitonic Subsequence\n\ndef maxLengthBitonicSubsequence(arr):\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    lis = [1] * n\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] &gt; arr[j] and lis[i] &lt; lis[j] + 1:\n                lis[i] = lis[j] + 1\n\n    lds = [1] * n\n    for i in range(n-2, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\n            if arr[i] &gt; arr[j] and lds[i] &lt; lds[j] + 1:\n                lds[i] = lds[j] + 1\n\n    max_length = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        max_length = max(max_length, lis[i] + lds[i] - 1)\n\n    return max_length\n\narr = [1, 11, 2, 10, 4, 5, 2, 1]\nprint(maxLengthBitonicSubsequence(arr))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Building Bridges\n\ndef buildingBridges(pairs):\n    pairs.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n\n    second_coordinate = [p[1] for p in pairs]\n\n    n = len(second_coordinate)\n    lis = [1] * n\n\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if second_coordinate[i] &gt; second_coordinate[j] and lis[i] &lt; lis[j] + 1:\n                lis[i] = lis[j] + 1\n\n    return max(lis)\n\npairs = [(8, 1), (1, 2), (4, 3), (3, 4), (6, 5), (5, 6)]\nprint(buildingBridges(pairs))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># The Longest Chain\n\ndef longestChain(pairs):\n    pairs.sort()\n\n    n = len(pairs)\n    dp = [1] * n\n\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if pairs[i][0] &gt; pairs[j][1] and dp[i] &lt; dp[j] + 1:\n                dp[i] = dp[j] + 1\n\n    return max(dp)\n\npairs = [(5, 24), (15, 25), (27, 40), (50, 60)]\nprint(longestChain(pairs))</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Minimum coins to make a value</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> coin : [25, 10, 5]; val = 30<br><strong>Output:</strong> 2</p>\n</div>\n<h4>Idea for the recursive Solution</h4>\n<p> Do following for every coin, coin[i]</p>\n<p> if coin[i] &gt; val</p>\n<p> -&gt; ignore the coin</p>\n<p> else</p>\n<p> -&gt; recursively call for val-coin[i] as new value</p>\n<p> -&gt; update the result is required</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursive\n\ndef mCoins(coin, val):\n  if val == 0:\n    return 0\n\n  n = len(coin)\n  res = -1\n\n  for i in range(n):\n    if coin[i] &lt;= val:\n      sub_res = mCoins(coin, val-coin[i])\n\n      if sub_res != -1:\n        if res == -1 or (sub_res+1) &lt; res:\n          res = sub_res + 1\n\n  return res</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef mCoinsDP(coin, val):\n    dp = [float('inf')] * (val + 1)\n\n    dp[0] = 0\n\n    for i in range(1, val + 1):\n        for c in coin:\n            if c &lt;= i:\n                dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[i - c] + 1)\n\n    return dp[val] if dp[val] != float('inf') else -1\n\ncoin = [1, 2, 5]\nval = 11\nprint(mCoinsDP(coin, val))</code></pre></figure></div><div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> arr = [3, 4, 2, 1, 2, 1]<br><strong>Output:</strong> 2</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursive\n\nimport sys\n\ndef minJumps(arr, n):\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n\n    res = sys.maxsize\n\n    for i in range(n-1):\n        if i + arr[i] &gt;= n - 1:\n            sub_res = minJumps(arr, i + 1)\n            if sub_res != sys.maxsize:\n                res = min(res, sub_res + 1)\n\n    return res\n\narr = [2, 3, 1, 1, 2, 4, 2, 0, 1, 1]\nn = len(arr)\n\nminJumps(arr, n)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef minJumpsDP(arr):\n  n = len(arr)\n  dp = [sys.maxsize] * n\n  dp[0] = 0\n\n  for i in range(1, n):\n    for j in range(i):\n      if (i &lt;= j + arr[j]) and (dp[j] != sys.maxsize):\n        dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n        break\n\n  return dp[n-1]\n\narr = [2, 3, 1, 1, 2, 4, 2, 0, 1, 1]\nminJumpsDP(arr)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>0-1 Knapsack Problems</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> V = [10, 40, 30, 50]; W = [5, 4, 6, 3]; w = 10<br><strong>Output:</strong> 90</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursion\n\ndef knapsack(W, wt, val, n):\n    if n == 0 or W == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    if wt[n-1] &gt; W:\n        return knapsack(W, wt, val, n-1)\n    else:\n        return max(\n            val[n-1] + knapsack(W - wt[n-1], wt, val, n-1),\n            knapsack(W, wt, val, n-1)\n        )\n\nval = [60, 100, 120]\nwt = [10, 20, 30]\nW = 50\nn = len(val)\n\nmax_value = knapsack(W, wt, val, n)\nprint(max_value)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef knapsack(W, wt, val, n):\n    dp = [[0 for _ in range(W + 1)] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        for w in range(W + 1):\n            if i == 0 or w == 0:\n                dp[i][w] = 0\n            elif wt[i-1] &lt;= w:\n                dp[i][w] = max(val[i-1] + dp[i-1][w-wt[i-1]], dp[i-1][w])\n            else:\n                dp[i][w] = dp[i-1][w]\n\n    return dp[n][W]\n\nval = [60, 100, 120]\nwt = [10, 20, 30]\nW = 50\nn = len(val)\n\nmax_value = knapsack(W, wt, val, n)\nprint(max_value)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Optimal Strategy for a Game</h3>\n<p> 1. Two Player Game</p>\n<p> 2. Both Players get turns one by one</p>\n<p> 3. Given no.of coins is even initially</p>\n<p> 4. You always make the first move</p>\n<p> 5. Both players play optimally</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> arr = [2, 3, 15, 7]<br><strong>Output:</strong> 17</p>\n</div>\n<p> Idea for recursive solution</p>\n<p> 1. We find the sum of values before recursive calls</p>\n<p> 2. we make two recursive calls and take the minimum of two</p>\n<p> a. Pick the left corner coin</p>\n<p> b. Pick the right corner coin</p>\n<p> sum - optimal value that opponent can get with the remaining coins</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursive Solution 1\n\ndef maxVal(arr):\n    return mvRec(arr, 0, len(arr) - 1, sum(arr))\n\ndef mvRec(arr, i, j, total_sum):\n    if i == j:\n        return arr[i]\n    if i + 1 == j:\n        return max(arr[i], arr[j])\n\n    return max(\n        total_sum - mvRec(arr, i + 1, j, total_sum - arr[i]),\n        total_sum - mvRec(arr, i, j - 1, total_sum - arr[j])\n    )\n\narr = [2, 3, 1, 1, 2, 4, 2, 0, 1, 1]\nmax_value = maxVal(arr)\nprint(max_value)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursive Solution 2\n\ndef mv(arr, i, j):\n    if i == j:\n        return arr[i]\n    if i + 1 == j:\n        return max(arr[i], arr[j])\n\n    return max(\n        min(mv(arr, i + 2, j), mv(arr, i + 1, j - 1) + arr[i]),\n        min(mv(arr, i + 1, j - 1), mv(arr, i, j - 2)) + arr[j]\n    )\n\narr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nmv(arr, 0, len(arr) - 1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef maxValDP(arr):\n    n = len(arr)\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    for length in range(1, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - length + 1):\n            j = i + length - 1\n            if i == j:\n                dp[i][j] = arr[i]\n            elif i + 1 == j:\n                dp[i][j] = max(arr[i], arr[j])\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = max(\n                    arr[i] + min(dp[i + 2][j] if i + 2 &lt;= j else 0, dp[i + 1][j - 1]),\n                    arr[j] + min(dp[i + 1][j - 1], dp[i][j - 2] if j - 2 &gt;= i else 0)\n                )\n\n    return dp[0][n - 1]\n\narr = [2, 3, 1, 1, 2, 4, 2, 0, 1, 1]\nmaxValDP(arr)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Egg Dropping Puzzle</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> e = 1, f = 10<br><strong>Output:</strong> 10<br><strong>Input:</strong> e = 2, f = 10<br><strong>Output:</strong> 4</p>\n</div>\n<p> Rules :</p>\n<p> -&gt; Egg may break from 1st floor</p>\n<p> -&gt; Egg may not break from the top floor</p>\n<p> -&gt; If an egg breaks from a floor it will break from higher floor also</p>\n<p> Find minimum Trails in wrost cases</p>\n<p> e -&gt; No.of Eggs</p>\n<p> f -&gt; No.of floors</p>\n<p> Let res(f, e) be the minimum trails in the wrost case for f floors and e eggs</p>\n<p> res(f, e) = (x=1 to f) Min(max(Breaks, Does not Break)) + 1</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursion Solution\n\ndef eggDrop(e, f):\n    if f == 0:\n        return 0\n    if f == 1:\n        return 1\n    if e == 1:\n        return f\n\n    min_trials = float('inf')\n\n    for x in range(1, f + 1):\n        breaks = eggDrop(e - 1, x - 1)\n        does_not_break = eggDrop(e, f - x)\n        worst_case = max(breaks, does_not_break)\n\n        min_trials = min(min_trials, worst_case + 1)\n\n    return min_trials\n\nprint(eggDrop(1, 10))\nprint(eggDrop(2, 10))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef eggDropDP(e, f):\n\n    dp = [[0 for _ in range(f + 1)] for _ in range(e + 1)]\n\n    for i in range(1, e + 1):\n        dp[i][0] = 0\n        dp[i][1] = 1\n\n    for j in range(1, f + 1):\n        dp[1][j] = j\n\n    for i in range(2, e + 1):\n        for j in range(2, f + 1):\n            dp[i][j] = float('inf')\n            for x in range(1, j + 1):\n                worst_case = 1 + max(dp[i - 1][x - 1], dp[i][j - x])\n                dp[i][j] = min(dp[i][j], worst_case)\n\n    return dp[e][f]\n\nprint(eggDropDP(1, 10))\nprint(eggDropDP(2, 10))</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Count BSTs with n Keys</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> n = 1<br><strong>Output:</strong> 1<br><strong>Input:</strong> n = 3<br><strong>Output:</strong> 5</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef countBST(n):\n  dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n  dp[0] = 1\n  dp[1] = 1\n\n  for i in range(2, n + 1):\n    for j in range(i):\n      dp[i] += dp[j] * dp[i - j - 1]\n\n  return dp[n]\n\ncountBST(3)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Maximum Sum with No Two consecutives</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> arr = [1, 10, 2]<br><strong>Output:</strong> 10<br><strong>Input:</strong> arr = [8, 7, 6, 10]<br><strong>Output:</strong> 18</p>\n</div>\n<h4>Idea for Naive Solution</h4>\n<p> maxSum(arr, n):</p>\n<p> We have two choices for every item:</p>\n<p> a. It is part of the result</p>\n<p> b. It is not part of the result</p>\n<p> We first consider the choices for the last item</p>\n<p> a. maxRec(arr, n-2) + arr[n-1]</p>\n<p> b. maxRec(arr, n-1)</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursive Solution\n\ndef maxSum(arr, n):\n  if n == 1:\n    return arr[0]\n\n  if n == 2:\n    return max(arr[0], arr[1])\n\n  return max(maxSum(arr, n-1), arr[n-1] + maxSum(arr, n-2))\n\narr = [8, 7, 6, 10]\nmaxSum(arr, len(arr))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef maxSum(arr):\n  n = len(arr)\n  dp = [None] * (n+1)\n  dp[1] = arr[0]\n  dp[2] = max(arr[0], arr[1])\n\n  for i in range(3, n+1):\n    dp[i] = max(dp[i-1], dp[i-2] + arr[i-1])\n\n  return dp[n]\n\narr = [8, 7, 6, 10]\nmaxSum(arr)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Optimized DP Solution\n\ndef maxSum(arr):\n  prev_p = arr[0]\n  prev = max(arr[0], arr[1])\n  res = prev\n\n  for i in range(3, len(arr)+1):\n    res = max(prev, prev_p + arr[i-1])\n    prev_p = prev\n    prev = res\n\n  return res\n\narr = [8, 7, 6, 10]\nmaxSum(arr)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Subset Sum problem</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef countSubsets(arr, target_sum):\n    n = len(arr)\n    dp = [[0 for x in range(target_sum + 1)] for x in range(n + 1)]\n\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        dp[i][0] = 1\n\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        for j in range(1, target_sum + 1):\n            if arr[i - 1] &gt; j:\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j]\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j] + dp[i - 1][j - arr[i - 1]]\n\n    return dp[n][target_sum]\n\narr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]\ntarget_sum = 10\ncountSubsets(arr, target_sum)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Matrix Chain Multiplication</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> arr = [2, 1, 3]<br><strong>Output:</strong> 6</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursion Solution\n\ndef mchain(arr, i, j):\n    if i + 1 == j:\n        return 0\n\n    res = float('inf')\n\n    for k in range(i + 1, j):\n        cost = mchain(arr, i, k) + mchain(arr, k, j) + arr[i] * arr[k] * arr[j]\n        res = min(res, cost)\n\n    return res\n\narr = [2, 1, 3]\nmchain(arr, 0, len(arr) - 1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef mchain(arr):\n    n = len(arr)\n    dp = [[0 for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]\n\n    for l in range(2, n):\n        for i in range(1, n - l + 1):\n            j = i + l - 1\n            dp[i][j] = float('inf')\n            for k in range(i, j):\n                q = dp[i][k] + dp[k + 1][j] + arr[i - 1] * arr[k] * arr[j]\n                if q &lt; dp[i][j]:\n                    dp[i][j] = q\n\n    return dp[1][n - 1]\n\narr = [2, 1, 3]\nmchain(arr)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Palindrome Partioning</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> str = \"geek\"<br><strong>Output:</strong> 2</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Base Case : If whole string is palindrome then return 0\n\ndef ispalin(s, i, j):\n    while i &lt; j:\n        if s[i] != s[j]:\n            return False\n        i += 1\n        j -= 1\n    return True\n\ndef palpat(s, i, j):\n    if ispalin(s, i, j):\n        return 0\n\n    res = float('inf')\n\n    for k in range(i, j):\n        cuts = 1 + palpat(s, i, k) + palpat(s, k + 1, j)\n        res = min(res, cuts)\n\n    return res\n\ns = \"abbac\"\nn = len(s)\npalpat(s, 0, n - 1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef minCuts(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    dp = [0] * n\n    palin = [[False] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    for j in range(n):\n        min_cuts = float('inf')\n        for i in range(j + 1):\n            if s[i] == s[j] and (j - i &lt;= 2 or palin[i + 1][j - 1]):\n                palin[i][j] = True\n                if i == 0:\n                    min_cuts = 0\n                else:\n                    min_cuts = min(min_cuts, dp[i - 1] + 1)\n        dp[j] = min_cuts\n\n    return dp[n - 1]\n\ns = \"abbac\"\nminCuts(s)</code></pre></figure>\n<h3>Allocate Minimum pages</h3>\n<p>dp[i][j] = minimum pages for i people and j books</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># DP Solution\n\ndef minPages(arr, n, k):\n    dp = [[float('inf')] * (n + 1) for _ in range(k + 1)]\n\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        dp[1][i] = sum(arr[0:i])\n\n    for i in range(1, k + 1):\n        dp[i][1] = arr[0]\n\n    for i in range(2, k + 1):\n        for j in range(2, n + 1):\n            for p in range(1, j + 1):\n                dp[i][j] = min(dp[i][j], max(dp[i - 1][p], sum(arr[p - 1:j])))\n\n    return dp[k][n]\n\narr = [12, 34, 67, 90]\nn = len(arr)\nk = 2\nminPages(arr, n, k)</code></pre></figure></div>"}